//huggingface.co/ibm-granite/granite-timeseries-ttm-r2","license":"Apache-2.0","citation":"Granite TimeSeries TTM r2 (IBM Granite, Apache-2.0) zero-shot forecast on daily flood-event counts at the nearest FloodNet ultrasonic depth sensor.","doc_id":"floodnet_forecast","date_modified":"live","retrieved_at":"live"},"fallback":{"on_offline":"skip","message":"TTM FloodNet forecast offline."}},{"id":"npcc4_slr","type":"baked","title":"NPCC4 (2024) sea-level rise projections","stone":"lodestone","tier":"modeled","maturity":"production","scope":"point","display":{"order":50,"kind":"stat","variant":"tabular","map_layer":false,"icon":"🌊"},"narration":{"short":"NYC Panel on Climate Change (2024) sea-level rise projections — Battery, NY.","template":"{narrative}"},"provenance":{"source_name":"NPCC4 — New York City Panel on Climate Change (2024)","source_url":"https://nyaspubs.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/nyas.15116","license":"CC-BY-NC 4.0 (Annals of the New York Academy of Sciences)","citation":"Rosenzweig, C. et al. (eds.). New York City Panel on Climate Change 2024 Report (NPCC4). Annals of the New York Academy of Sciences, Vol. 1539 (parent doi:10.1111/nyas.15116). Sea-level rise projections from the NPCC4 climate-science chapter — Battery (NOAA station 8518750) reference.","doc_id":"npcc4_slr","date_modified":"2024","retrieved_at":"2026-07-11"},"fallback":{"on_offline":"error","message":"NPCC4 SLR table missing."}}]}`),d=JSON.parse(`{"query":"560 Grand Street, Manhattan, NY","intent":"single_address","plan":{"intent":"single_address","rationale":"Heuristic match: single_address.","targets":[{"type":"address","text":"560 Grand Street, Manhattan, NY"}],"place":{"kind":"address","text":"560 Grand Street, Manhattan, NY","certain":true,"message":null}},"geocode":{"address":"560, Grand Street, East River Cooperative, Manhattan Community Board 3, Manhattan, New York County, New York, 10002, United States","borough":"Manhattan","lat":40.7139786,"lon":-73.9796966,"bbl":null,"bin":null,"match":"exact"},"lat":40.7139786,"lon":-73.9796966,"nta":null,"policy_corpus":{"query":"address 560, Grand Street, East River Cooperative, Manhattan Community Board 3, Manhattan, New York County, New York, 10002, United States; in Manhattan; inside Hurricane Sandy 2012 inundation zone; flood resilience plan, vulnerability, hardening, mitigation","rag_hits":[{"doc_id":"rag_nycha","title":"Flood Resilience at NYCHA — Lessons Learned","citation":"NYCHA, Flood Resilience: Lessons Learned","file":"nycha_lessons.pdf","page":3,"text":"Letter from the Vice President for Recovery & Resilience Dear New Yorkers, Hurricane Sandy devasted New York City in October 2012. Despite living in a city with 520 miles of coastline, residents had never grappled with storm surge at this scale. Salt water and debris covered huge swaths of the five boroughs, including many NYCHA campuses. Unlike prior major storms, the impacts didn’t dissipate when the weather improved. Salt water corroded critical infrastructure that was stored below grade. The homes of tens of thousands of public housing residents—most of whom couldn’t evacuate before the storm—were without water, power, heat, hot water, or elevator service for weeks.","score":0.8563995361328125}],"entities":{"nycha":{"rag_doc_id":"rag_nycha","title":"Flood Resilience at NYCHA — Lessons Learned","paragraph_excerpt":"Letter from the Vice President for Recovery & Resilience Dear New Yorkers, Hurricane Sandy devasted New York City in October 2012. Despite living in a city with 520 miles of coastline, residents had never grappled with storm surge at this s…","n_entities":6,"entities":[{"label":"agency","text":"New Yorkers","score":0.848},{"label":"infrastructure_project","text":"Hurricane Sandy","score":0.997},{"label":"nyc_location","text":"New York City","score":0.835},{"label":"date_range","text":"October 2012","score":0.947},{"label":"agency","text":"NYCHA","score":0.917},{"label":"date_range","text":"weeks","score":0.985}]}},"n_hits":1,"n_entities":6},"dep":{"dep_extreme_2080":{"depth_class":0,"depth_label":"outside","citation":"NYC DEP Stormwater Flood Map — Extreme (3.66 in/hr, 2080 SLR)","narrative":"This address is outside the modeled flooding in the NYC DEP stormwater scenario (3.66 in/hr, 2080 SLR)."},"dep_moderate_2050":{"depth_class":0,"depth_label":"outside","citation":"NYC DEP Stormwater Flood Map — Moderate (2.13 in/hr, 2050 SLR)","narrative":"This address is outside the modeled flooding in the NYC DEP stormwater scenario (2.13 in/hr, 2050 SLR)."},"dep_moderate_current":{"depth_class":0,"depth_label":"outside","citation":"NYC DEP Stormwater Flood Map — Moderate (2.13 in/hr, current SLR)","narrative":"This address is outside the modeled flooding in the NYC DEP stormwater scenario (2.13 in/hr, current sea level, near-term)."}},"paragraph":"This is an automated hazard-exposure briefing produced by Riprap from live and baked data sources. It is informational only and not a substitute for a professional risk assessment.\\n\\n**In brief.**\\nThis address is outside the 2012 Sandy inundation footprint [sandy_inundation], in FEMA flood zone X [fema_nfhl], and outside the modeled flooding in the DEP stormwater scenarios for current, 2050 and 2080 sea-level rise [dep_moderate_current][dep_moderate_2050][dep_extreme_2080]. 15 flood-related 311 complaints were filed within 200 m in the last 5 years [nyc311].\\n\\n**Hazard Reader.**\\nThis address sits outside the empirical 2012 Hurricane Sandy inundation footprint (NYC OEM) [sandy_inundation]. This address sits in FEMA flood zone X, per NFHL FIRM panel 3604970203F, effective 2007 [fema_nfhl]. NYC DEP stormwater scenarios at this address: current sea level with 2.13 in/hr of rain, outside the modeled flooding [dep_moderate_current]; 2050 sea-level rise with 2.13 in/hr, outside the modeled flooding [dep_moderate_2050]; 2080 sea-level rise with 3.66 in/hr, outside the modeled flooding [dep_extreme_2080]. No Hurricane Ida (Sept 2021) high-water marks were surveyed within 800 m of this address [ida_hwm]. Nearest USGS-surveyed mark: South of Bedford Ave., between north 12th St. and Lorimer Ave., in McCarren Park, Brooklyn (2361 m away) [ida_hwm]. Experimental: msradam/Prithvi-EO-2.0-NYC-Pluvial on Sentinel-2 scenes from 2021-09-02, compared with 2021-08-13: 0 m² of new surface water within 500 m of this address (100% of that circle observed); 0.00% of Lower East Side showed new water (100% of it observed) [prithvi_water]. Street and basement flooding drains within hours and is not visible at 10 to 20 m, so no water here is not evidence of no flooding [prithvi_water]. Elevation 5.82 m; this point is higher than 67% of the ground within 200 m (the 67th percentile; a low percentile means a local low spot where water collects); HAND (height above the nearest drainage channel) 5.82 m; TWI (topographic wetness index; higher means water tends to collect) 8.49 [microtopo]. Local basin relief (the rise from this point to the highest ground within 750 m): 5.24 m [microtopo]. Experimental: Policy passages retrieved from the NYC flood-policy corpus for this place: 1 (embedding retrieval; entity tags are experimental) [policy_corpus].\\n\\n**Live Observer.**\\n2 FloodNet community sensors within 600 m have logged 6 above-curb flood events in the last 3 years [floodnet]. Peak depth recorded by these sensors: 96 mm [floodnet]. 15 NYC 311 flood-related complaints filed within 200 m of this location in the last 5 years: 5 sewer backup, 5 catch basin, 4 street flooding, 1 manhole overflow [nyc311]. Latest reading at The Battery, NY: 5.92 ft above MLLW (+1.04 ft vs predicted tide), observed 2026-09-28 21:30 [noaa_tides]. No active USGS stream gauge reported a stage reading in the last 2 days within about 10 to 14 km of this address [usgs_gauges].\\n\\n**Asset Register.**\\n0 MTA subway entrances within 800 m of this address: 0 inside the 2012 Sandy inundation extent and 0 inside the DEP extreme stormwater scenario (2080 sea-level rise) [mta_entrance_exposure]. 5 NYCHA developments within 2000 m of this address: 3 inside the 2012 Sandy inundation extent and 2 inside the DEP extreme stormwater scenario (2080 sea-level rise) [nycha_development_exposure]. 3 NYC DOE schools within 1500 m of this address: 3 inside the 2012 Sandy inundation extent and 0 inside the DEP extreme stormwater scenario (2080 sea-level rise) [doe_school_exposure]. 5 hospitals within 3000 m of this address: 0 inside the 2012 Sandy inundation extent and 2 inside the DEP extreme stormwater scenario (2080 sea-level rise) [doh_hospital_exposure].\\n\\n**Projector.**\\nNo active NWS flood / coastal / wind alerts at this point [nws_alerts]. Experimental: a TTM model fine-tuned on Battery gauge history, with no wind or pressure input, forecasts a peak surge residual of 0.37 m at The Battery about 5 h ahead [ttm_battery_surge]. Use NOAA ETSS or the Stevens Flood Advisory System for storm decisions. Experimental: 3 flood-related 311 complaints within 200 m over the last 512 days; a zero-shot TTM forecast with no rainfall input and no backtest projects about 0.04 per week for the next 96 days [ttm_311_forecast]. NPCC4 (2024) projects sea-level rise at the Battery of 0.38 m (15 in) by the 2050s and 0.79 m (31 in) by 2100 at the median, relative to 2000-2004; 90th percentile 0.74 m and 1.65 m [npcc4_slr].\\n\\n**Out of scope.** This briefing does not assess title, structural condition, or compliance with specific zoning rules. Where a probe was offline at run time, the relevant section omits that signal.","audit":{"raw":"This is an automated hazard-exposure briefing produced by Riprap from live and baked data sources. It is informational only and not a substitute for a professional risk assessment.\\n\\n**In brief.**\\nThis address is outside the 2012 Sandy inundation footprint [sandy_inundation], in FEMA flood zone X [fema_nfhl], and outside the modeled flooding in the DEP stormwater scenarios for current, 2050 and 2080 sea-lev